## 4. Architecture and Separation of Responsibilities

Build a decoupled application with separate frontend and backend applications.

The architecture must follow this flow:

React frontend
→ HTTP REST API
→ Express routes and middleware
→ Controllers and service layer
→ File storage and MongoDB

### Frontend responsibilities

- Render the document dashboard.
- Allow users to select or drag and drop a file.
- Show the accepted file formats and maximum file size.
- Display upload progress or processing status.
- Fetch and render document metadata.
- Trigger downloads.
- Confirm and trigger deletion.
- Show clear notifications and error states.

### Backend responsibilities

- Expose REST API endpoints.
- Validate request parameters and uploaded files.
- Use Multer to process file uploads.
- Extract and validate text contents where applicable.
- Manage file storage.
- Persist and retrieve document metadata through Mongoose.
- Handle deletion and partial failures.
- Return predictable HTTP status codes and JSON error responses.

### Storage responsibilities

Keep actual file contents separate from MongoDB metadata.

- Store actual documents in a dedicated local filesystem directory.
- Store document metadata and storage references in MongoDB.
- Keep temporary uploads separate from permanent files.
- Never expose internal filesystem paths in public API responses.
- Never serve the storage directory as a publicly accessible static directory.

Use a storage service or utility module so filesystem operations are not scattered throughout the controllers. The design should make it possible to replace local storage with an object-storage provider in the future without rewriting the React application.

